Fitting the \(p_{T}\) spectra is a vital part of the analysis, as it enables extrapolations to be made over the low \(p_{T}\) region of the spectra where there was no acceptance. However, in order to believe the extrapolation the fit must have some theoretical basis. In heavy ion analyses it is common to use an \(m_{T}\) exponential function inspired by a thermal description of the emitting source [35],

\[\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}N}{dy\,dp_{T}}=A\,e^{\frac{-m_{T}}{T}} \tag{6.3}\]

where \(A\) is a constant and \(T\) is the temperature. There is also the pQCD inspired power law function described by equation 2.3. Hagedorn showed that equation 2.3 may be re-written as [35],

\[\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}N}{dy\,dp_{T}}=B\left(1+\frac{2p_{T}}{(n-3){<}p_ {T}{>}}\right)^{-n} \tag{6.4}\]

where \(B\) and \(n\) are constants. The UA1 experiment observed that, for \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) the \(p_{T}\) spectra is harder than that of \(K_{S}^{0}\), with the effect of pushing \(n\) in the power law (equation 6.4) to \(\infty\). This was also observed for this analysis and so, therefore, in effect an exponential in \(p_{T}\), as shown in equation 6.5 was used to fit the \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) spectra,

\[\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}\,N}{dy\,dp_{T}}=A\,e^{\frac{-p_{T}}{T}} \tag{6.5}\]

As pointed out by Hagedorn [35], spectra may contain contributions from both thermal emission, represented by equation 6.3 and the pQCD inspired power law model represented by equation 6.4. As such a composite equation is represented by,

\[\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}N}{dy\,dp_{T}}=A\,e^{\frac{-m_{T}}{T}}+B{\left( 1+\frac{2p_{T}}{(n-3){<}p_{T}{>}}\right)^{-n}} \tag{6.6}\]

as for the \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\)\(n\rightarrow\infty\) and equation 6.6 becomes,

\[\frac{1}{2\pi p_{T}}\frac{d^{2}\,N}{dy\,dp_{T}}=A\,e^{\frac{-m_{T}}{T}}+Be^{ \frac{-p_{T}}{T}} \tag{6.7}\]

