\(\Lambda\) or \(\overline{\Lambda}\) being \(\sim\) 22 times more abundant in high multiplicity collisions (\(>\) 13 tracks), compared to low multiplicity collisions (\(0\to 2\) tracks). The lighter \(K^{0}_{S}\) is only 15 times more abundant in high multiplicity collisions, compared to collisions of the lowest multiplicity class, suggesting that \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) production in the lowest multiplicity class is suppressed due to their larger mass. Interestingly the ratio of \(\overline{\Lambda}\) to \(\Lambda\) increases from 0.86 in the lowest multiplicity class (\(0\to 2\)), to 0.99 in the \(>13\) class, suggesting that the relative proportions of \(q\overline{q}\) pairs compared to original valence quarks (at y=0) grow with multiplicity. This may be caused by harder collisions at the partonic level, which in turn might create more energetic (or 'longer') strings, which then fragment into more \(q\overline{q}\) pairs.

The behaviour of \(\Lambda\), \(\overline{\Lambda}\) and \(K^{0}_{S}\)\(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) as a function of multiplicity is shown in Figure 7.7. As for the UA1 results (Figure 2.5) the same trend of increasing \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) as a function of multiplicity is observed. However, where as the UA1 (and for the \(K^{0}_{S}\), E735) results indicate a linear relationship between \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) and multiplicity, in Figure 7.7 there is, perhaps, the hint of a'step' in the relationship between \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) and multiplicity for \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\), at around 7 primary tracks. Also the \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) increase for the \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) does not appear to be as steep as the trend observed by UA1.

The overall value of \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) obtained for the \(K^{0}_{S}\) by UA1 (p + \(\overline{\rm p}\) at \(\sqrt{s}\) = 630 GeV) is less than measured by this analysis. Table 7.4 shows a comparison with the (\(\Lambda\) +\(\overline{\Lambda}\)) and \(K^{0}_{S}\)\(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) as measured by the UA5 collaboration, and the \(K^{0}_{S}\)\(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) is again shown to be less than the measured value. A possible explanation may be in the fact that both UA1 and UA5 fit power laws (equation 2.3) to their \(K^{0}_{S}\) spectra, which has (as shown in Table 6.4) the effect of reducing \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\).

It is revealing to briefly compare the behaviour of \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) with the trends observedin high energy Au + Au collisions. For relativistic heavy ion collisions, multiplicity is used as a measure of collision centrality (see Appendix A), and for Au + Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s}=130\) GeV both \(\frac{\overline{\Lambda}}{\Lambda}\) and the Hagedorn temperature parameter, T (\(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) ), were observed to be approximately constant with multiplicity [78]. Given that a single p + p collision may consist of multiple parton + parton interactions [43], it is not unreasonable to consider that multiplicity may have also been related to the 'centrality' of a p + p collision. However, the observations of increasing \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) and \(\frac{\overline{\Lambda}}{\Lambda}\) with multiplicity in p + p collisions strongly suggest that the increase in multiplicity is caused by harder interactions between the constituent partons, unlike for high energy heavy ion collisions.

### 7.2 Comparisons with Models

As discussed in Chapter 2, comparison with various model predictions remains a good reason for studying the p + p data in its own right. Calculations based on next to leading order pQCD have been made for the \(K_{S}^{0}\) spectra by Albino, Kniehl and Kramer (AKK)[83], and also Kniehl, Kramer and Potter (KKP) [84]. Figure 7.8 compares the pQCD calculations with the measured \(K_{S}^{0}\) spectra. Even at high \(p_{T}\) there are discrepancies between the calculations and the data, with the KKP results in particular favouring the UA1 data. However, the \(K_{S}^{0}\) from this analysis appear to achieve better agreement with the more recent AKK calculation compared to the KKP prediction.

Dumitru has also studied \(\rm p+p\) collisions within the context of the PYTHIA model [52]. Dumitru states that at RHIC energies, the colour flux tubes may no longer be orientated longitudinally (as in non-pQCD processes), but instead acquire significant transverse momentum, due to perturbative interactions. Although hadrons are still being produced via a fragmentation mechanism, the fragmenting strings can be rotated with respect to the lab frame, and can be regarded as'mini jets'. Therefore this picture is consistent

